.TH TURBOJEDI 1 "October 2026" "TurboJedi" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
turbojedi \- Turbo Vision viewer of the systemd journal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B turbojedi
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIFIELD\fB=\fIVALUE\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B turbojedi
shows the systemd journal in windows and follows it.
Every window is a journal client of its own: its filter goes to the journal as a query.
Options set the filter of the first window, the dialogs of the Journal menu change the filter of the focused one.
.PP
\fIFIELD\fB=\fIVALUE\fR matches any journal field.
Matches of the same field are joined by OR, of different fields by AND.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-identifier\fR \fIID\fR
Entries of SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER \fIID\fR. Repeatable.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-unit\fR \fIUNIT\fR
Entries of systemd unit \fIUNIT\fR, \fB.service\fR is assumed without a suffix. Repeatable.
.TP
\fB\-U\fR, \fB\-\-user\fR \fIUSER\fR
Entries of user \fIUSER\fR, by name or number. Repeatable.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-priority\fR \fILEVEL\fR
Entries up to \fILEVEL\fR: emerg, alert, crit, err, warning, notice, info, debug or 0..7.
.TP
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-kernel\fR
Kernel messages only.
.TP
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-since\fR \fITIME\fR
Entries from \fITIME\fR on instead of the last ones.
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fITIME\fR
Entries up to \fITIME\fR, the window stops following then.
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-boot\fR
Entries of the current boot only.
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-directory\fR \fIDIR\fR
Journal files of \fIDIR\fR instead of the local journal, in the first window.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-merge\fR
Journals of every machine there are, like \fBjournalctl \-\-merge\fR: other machine IDs in
\fI/var/log/journal\fR and \fI/var/log/journal/remote\fR of
.BR systemd-journal-remote (8),
instead of this machine only.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-lines\fR \fIN\fR
History to start with, 1000 by default.
.TP
\fB\-H\fR, \fB\-\-highlight\fR \fINAME\fR
Rules that paint messages: \fBbuiltin\fR (default), \fBoff\fR, a rule file of
.BR grc (1)
such as \fBconf.log\fR from \fI~/.grc\fR, \fI/usr/local/share/grc\fR or \fI/usr/share/grc\fR, or a path to one.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-layout\fR \fIFILE\fR
Windows of a layout saved by File, Save layout. Options are left for new windows then.
.TP
\fB\-\-clipboard\-copy\fR \fICOMMAND\fR
Command of
.BR sh (1)
that takes copied lines on its standard input instead of the clipboard of the terminal, such as
\fBremote-copy.sh\fR of RemoteClipboard. It goes over \fBTURBOJEDI_CLIPBOARD_COPY\fR. When the command exits
with an error or does not finish in 3 seconds, the clipboard of the terminal takes the lines.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Usage.
.PP
\fITIME\fR is \fB2026-10-23 19:00\fR[\fB:00\fR], \fB2026-10-23\fR, \fB19:00\fR (today), \fBnow\fR, \fBtoday\fR,
\fByesterday\fR, \fBtomorrow\fR or relative \fB\-1h\fR, \fB+30m\fR (s, m, h, d).
.SH KEYS
.TP 18
.B F1
Keys.
.TP
.B F2
New window with the filter of the focused one.
.TP
.B F3
Levels.
.TP
.B F4
Text filter: plain or regular expression, to show or to leave out the matching entries, case sensitive or not.
.TP
.B F7
Source: unit, identifier, command, transport, boot, user, machine, PID, with lists of the values in the journal.
.TP
.B Shift-F7
Journals of the window: of this machine, of every machine there is, like \fBjournalctl \-\-merge\fR, or of a directory.
.TP
.B Ctrl-T
Time range.
.TP
.B Ctrl-K
Columns: date, time, level, host, unit, identifier, PID; UTC, milliseconds, wrapped messages.
.TP
.B Ctrl-W
Wrap long messages.
.TP
.B Ctrl-L
Highlighting rules of the window.
.TP
.B Ctrl-F
Find and highlight. \fBCtrl-N\fR and \fBCtrl-P\fR go to the next and previous one,
the search goes on into the older entries above the first line.
.TP
.B Ctrl-G
Go to a time, given the way \fB\-\-since\fR takes it.
.TP
.B Alt-G
Every other window goes to the time of the selected line, their filters stay as they are.
.TP
.B Alt-Left
Back to where the window was before \fBCtrl-G\fR, \fBAlt-G\fR or a find, also \fBAlt-Backspace\fR.
.TP
.B Alt-T
Timeline: entries of the filter of the window by intervals of time, errors, warnings and the rest stacked
in a column per interval. \fBLeft\fR and \fBRight\fR move the cursor, \fBEnter\fR takes every journal window
to the first entry of the interval, \fB+\fR and \fB\-\fR make the intervals finer or coarser,
\fBL\fR switches between linear and logarithmic scale.
.TP
.B Enter
All fields of the entry, also by a double click.
\fBEnter\fR on a field there opens a window with the filter of this one and the field.
.TP
.B Space
Pause or follow the tail. Scrolling above the first line brings the older entries in.
.TP
.B Shift+arrows
Select lines, also with the mouse. \fBEsc\fR drops the selection.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Ins
Copy the selected lines or the screen to the clipboard.
.TP
.B Ctrl-E
Export the selected lines or all of them to a file.
.TP
.B F8
Clear the window.
.TP
.B Ctrl-O\fR, \fBCtrl-S
Open, save the layout of all windows.
.TP
.B Alt-X
Exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TURBOJEDI_CLIPBOARD_COPY
Command that takes copied lines, like \fB\-\-clipboard\-copy\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/turbojedi/last.jedi
The layout kept on exit, \fI~/.config/turbojedi/last.jedi\fR without XDG_CONFIG_HOME.
It is opened on the next start unless a layout or filter options are given.
.SH NOTES
Reading the whole journal needs root or the \fBsystemd-journal\fR group.
.SH AUTHOR
Artem Prilutskiy, https://github.com/cyanide-burnout/TurboJedi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR grc (1),
.BR sd-journal (3)
